# Role and Goal
You are building a production landing page for a real business owner (master Angelika) and portfolio usage.
Every change must improve business value, trust, conversion, performance, and maintainability.

# Core Product Constraints
- Landing is a real business asset, not a demo.
- Tone: premium, trustworthy, clear, concise.
- Prioritize conversion and clarity over visual complexity.
- Content must support two goals:
  1) bring real clients to Angelika;
  2) present strong portfolio quality for developer showcase.

# Architecture and Code Quality
- Keep modular structure: sections/components/services/utils separated by responsibility.
- No monolithic files.
- Reusable UI blocks and shared primitives are required.
- Prefer explicit typing for exported APIs and props.
- Avoid `any` unless no safe alternative exists.
- Keep functions small and focused.
- Use descriptive naming in English for code identifiers.
- Remove dead code and avoid duplicate logic.

# UI and UX Requirements
- Mobile-first responsive layout (320px+).
- Clear hierarchy: hero -> trust proof -> services -> process -> reviews -> CTA -> contacts.
- Every screen should have a clear next action.
- Buttons and forms must have obvious states (default, hover, active, disabled, loading, error, success).
- Accessibility is mandatory:
  - semantic HTML;
  - keyboard navigation;
  - visible focus states;
  - alt text for meaningful images;
  - sufficient color contrast.

# Conversion Standards
- Primary CTA visible in first screen.
- Repeated CTA blocks throughout the page.
- Contact methods are frictionless (messenger, phone, social links).
- Trust elements are mandatory:
  - real photos and works;
  - testimonials;
  - social proof;
  - transparent process;
  - clear pricing logic (or pricing format explanation).
- Avoid generic marketing fluff; use concrete client-facing value.

# Content and Brand Rules
- Brand owner is Angelika.
- Content voice should match personal expert brand (human, warm, professional).
- No fake claims, no fake reviews, no placeholder legal text in production.
- Keep copy concise and benefit-oriented.
- If text is placeholder, explicitly mark it as placeholder in comments or content notes.

# Performance and SEO
- Optimize all media (modern formats, responsive sizes).
- Avoid heavy libraries without real need.
- Ensure good Core Web Vitals targets (LCP, CLS, INP awareness).
- Technical SEO baseline:
  - unique title and meta description;
  - proper heading structure (single H1);
  - Open Graph tags;
  - favicon and social preview image;
  - robots and sitemap if project scope includes them.

# Forms and Data Handling
- Validate all user inputs on client and server boundaries (if backend exists).
- Never trust raw external input.
- Show clear validation and submission feedback.
- Do not expose secrets in client code.
- If integrations exist (CRM, Telegram, email), isolate integration logic in dedicated modules or services.

# Visual Consistency
- Use a consistent design token approach:
  - spacing scale;
  - typography scale;
  - color roles;
  - border radius and shadows.
- Keep spacing rhythm consistent between sections.
- Avoid random one-off values unless justified.
- Prefer component variants over ad-hoc inline styling.

# Delivery Discipline
- Keep changes scoped to requested task.
- Do not add unrelated features.
- Do not add dependencies unless justified by clear business or technical need.
- Before finalizing, verify:
  - no obvious console errors;
  - no broken layout on mobile, tablet, desktop;
  - no regressions in key CTA flows.

# Collaboration Output Style
When generating implementation proposals:
1. First list files to be created or changed.
2. State assumptions explicitly.
3. Provide minimal scalable solution.
4. Mark placeholders explicitly.
5. After completing code changes, always provide a suggested commit title (short, imperative, and specific).
